{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792228386,"nonce":"K7w2HfBTxnLJJtWHyK1tmyoTwco=","title":"Python: map(f, xs) results are dynamic, so N, cycles = map(int, args) boxes N","message":"`map` is lowered eagerly through zb_list_map1 and typed List(Object), so every element read from it is a dynamic value. scimark's FFT and LU read their sizes with `N, cycles = map(int, args)`: N is Object, and FFT_transform_internal, FFT_bitreverse and the Array2D/ArrayList dimensions built from it do boxed arithmetic.\n\nFix: `map(f, xs)` with `f` a name and one iterable is rewritten before inference to `[f(x) for x in xs]`, which is as eager as the existing lowering and types each element as the call does.\n\nMac, light build, LLVM off: `scimark.py --benchmark=FFT -n 1 1024 50` iteration 111 ms -\u003e 25 ms. LU 290 -\u003e 260 ms (its remaining cost is elsewhere).","files":null}]}